WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.073
Idea. You pay back ten dollars you borrowed from a friend. Your cash goes down, but are you poorer? No. You also owe ten dollars less. Profit works the same way: it only changes when income or an expense changes.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:31.379
More income or less expense raises profit. More expense or less income lowers it. Capital, drawings, buying a non-current asset, or paying a debt only move assets, liabilities and capital, so profit stays the same.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:45.965
Method. Write the double entry first. If neither account is income or an expense, there is no effect. If income rises or an expense falls, profit increases. The opposite, and profit decreases.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:01:00.284
For an error, compare profit now with what it should be. An expense left out, such as rent owing, makes profit too high: overstated. A prepayment ignored makes the expense too high, so profit is understated.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:13.403
Working capital is current assets minus current liabilities. It only changes if the total of either changes. Moving cash into the bank swaps two current assets, so there is no effect.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:26.335
Worked example. Here is a real question from November 2024. Which transaction would affect a business's profit? One mark. Write the double entry for each option.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:37.218
Option A: selling an asset at its book value. Debit bank, credit the asset, with the same amount. There is no gain and no loss, so no effect on profit.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:52.238
Option B: paying a supplier and receiving a discount. Debit the supplier with the full debt. Credit bank with the amount paid, and discount received with the rest. Discount received is income, so profit increases.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:02:08.299
Option C: paying a debt in full. Debit the supplier, credit bank. A liability and an asset both fall. Option D: cash paid into the bank. Debit bank, credit cash. Two assets swap. Neither changes profit.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:19.249
So the answer is B. It is the only option that touches an income account. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:29.085
Common mistake. A common mistake is thinking any cash going out lowers profit. Drawings reduce the owner's capital, not profit.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:36.860
Another is getting omissions backwards. If an expense was left out, profit is too high, so it is overstated.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:45.123
Your turn. Now you try. For each item, decide the effect on profit for the year. Pause and decide.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:03:01.643
Answer. Capital and the van only move assets and capital, so no effect. Interest received is income: an increase. Electricity is an expense: a decrease. Missing depreciation leaves profit too high: overstated.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:10.572
Remember: write the double entry, check for income or an expense, then decide the direction. Now try the practice questions.
